* cpu1: where a fast ADC channel rolls off.
*
* Measured as a step response rather than a frequency sweep. For a single-pole
* RC the two say the same thing - the time to 63.2 % of a step is RC, and the
* corner is 1/2*pi*RC - and ngspice's .meas can read a transient vector without
* ambiguity where an AC one gives its real part unless coaxed. The identity is
* the only thing taken on trust here, and it is one line of algebra.
*
* One RC, and the impedance the power board is required to present in front of
* it. That promise is part of the filter: a sensor with a hundred ohms of output
* impedance moves this corner by a decade with nothing on this board changing,
* which is why `header.source_impedance` is declared and read here.
*
* `checks/test_adc.py` computes the same corner from the same values. This is
* the second method, and it is worth having only while the two can disagree.
*
* Values come from the build; @path:end@ is an instance path and which end of
* its resolved range to take. Both are at the end that makes the corner lowest,
* which is the end that matters: too low and the loop measures a current that
* happened a moment ago.

Vin in 0 PULSE(0 1 0 10p 10p 5u 10u)
Rsource in src @header.source_impedance:max@
Rseries src pin @adc.fast1.series.resistance:max@
Cshunt pin 0 @adc.fast1.shunt.capacitance:max@

.tran 0.2n 2u

* The time constant, straight off the step, and the corner that follows from it.
.meas tran t_tau WHEN v(pin)=0.6321206 RISE=1
.meas tran f_corner PARAM='1/(6.283185307*t_tau)'

* And how long the pin takes to become a faithful copy - four and a half time
* constants to a tenth of a percent, which is the number that says how soon
* after a step the converter may be believed.
.meas tran t_settled WHEN v(pin)=0.999 RISE=1

.end
